{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.C1ThetaZeroLocus","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ThetaZeroLocus","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Order","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.C1ConnectionPoles"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_analyticOrderAt_neg_zpow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_analyticOrderAt_neg_zpow","kind":"theorem","line":495,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L495-L514","doc":"**Every zero of `θ_P` is simple.**\n\nTogether with `qThetaC_eq_zero_iff` this is the entire divisor of `θ_P` on\n`ℂ*`: the orbit `−P^{2ℤ}`, reduced. It is what makes the C1 pole divisor\n*determined* rather than merely supported — a containment of zero sets fixes\nwhere the poles can be, but not with what multiplicity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":433,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L433-L493","doc":"**The zero locus of `θ_P`, in full: `θ_P(w) = 0 ↔ w ∈ −P^{2ℤ}`.**\n\nThe converse of `qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero`, with no hypothesis beyond `w ≠ 0` —\nand `0` is excluded only because it is an essential singularity, where `θ_P` is\nnot an analytic object at all. With `p = P²` this is the classical statement\nthat the zero set of `θ_p` is exactly `−p^ℤ`, each zero simple.\n\nThe argument is the fundamental-annulus result plus arithmetic:\n`⌊logb (P²) ‖w‖⌋` names the annulus `P^{2k} ≤ ‖w‖ < P^{2k+2}` holding `w`;\n`exists_clean_radius` places a clean contour `ρ` in `(‖w‖/P², P^{2k})`, an\ninterval that is nonempty *because* `‖w‖ < P^{2k+2}`; and `(ρ, P²ρ)` then\ncontains both `w` and `−P^{2k}`, which\n`qThetaC_zero_locus_fundamental_annulus` identifies.\n\nThis is what makes the C1 pole divisor exact rather than a containment: every\nlocus in the table at the top of `C1ConnectionPoles.lean` is a single `p`-orbit\nof this set, with nothing else in it. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:c1-theta-zero-locus-and-pole-divisor","label":"prop:c1-theta-zero-locus-and-pole-divisor","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/c1-theta-zero-locus-and-pole-divisor.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_zero_locus_fundamental_annulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_zero_locus_fundamental_annulus","kind":"theorem","line":400,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L400-L427","doc":"**The converse zero locus on a fundamental annulus.**\n\nIf a lattice point `−P^{2k}` lies in the annulus, it *is* the unique zero — so\non that annulus the zero set of `θ_P` is exactly `{−P^{2k}}`, which is the\nconverse the C1 pole divisor needs.\n\nChoosing the `k` is arithmetic on `Real.log`, not analysis, and is left to the\ncaller: the annulus has ratio `P²` and the lattice spacing is `P²`, so an *open*\nfundamental annulus carries one lattice point or none, depending on where `ρ`\nfalls. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_unique_zero_fundamental_annulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_unique_zero_fundamental_annulus","kind":"theorem","line":352,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L352-L398","doc":"**`θ_P` has exactly one zero in each fundamental annulus.**\n\nThe converse of `qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero`, and the statement that makes the C1\npole divisor exact rather than `⊇`.\n\n`qTheta_winding_step` puts the winding difference across `ρ < ‖z‖ < P²ρ` at\nexactly `2πi`; `qThetaC_zeros_annulus` reads that difference as\n`(∑ m)·2πi`, so `∑ m = 1`; every multiplicity is at least `1`, so the index set\nis a single point.\n\nThe one hypothesis — no zero on the inner circle — is `qTheta_winding_step`'s\nown, and is unavoidable rather than restrictive: the integrand `θ'/θ` is not\ndefined on a circle through a zero, and the radius is ours to choose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_zeros_annulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_zeros_annulus","kind":"theorem","line":250,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L250-L342","doc":"**The zeros of `θ_P` between two clean circles, and the winding number that\ncounts them.**\n\nIf `θ_P` vanishes nowhere on `‖z‖ = r` or on `‖z‖ = R`, its zeros in the open\nannulus form a finite set `S` with strictly positive multiplicities `m`, and\n\n    ∮_{|z|=R} θ'/θ  −  ∮_{|z|=r} θ'/θ  =  (∑ a ∈ S, m a) · 2πi.\n\nThe factorisation is taken on an annulus strictly larger than the contours — it\nmust be, since the argument principle needs the non-vanishing cofactor on a\n*neighbourhood* of the closed contour annulus — and\n`argumentPrinciple_annulus_filter` counts only the factors in between.\n\nNothing here mentions `−P^{2ℤ}`: which points the zeros are is arithmetic, and\nis the caller's job. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.exists_clean_radius","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.exists_clean_radius","kind":"theorem","line":228,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L228-L244","doc":"**Every open interval of radii contains one on which `θ_P` does not vanish.**\n\nOnly finitely many radii carry a zero — the zeros in an annulus are finite — and\nan interval of reals is infinite.\n\nThis is what makes the \"no zero on `‖z‖ = ρ`\" hypothesis of `qTheta_winding_step`\nand everything downstream a choice rather than a restriction: given any point one\nwants to reason about, a clean contour can always be placed on either side of\nit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_zeros_iff_annulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_zeros_iff_annulus","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L203-L222","doc":"**The zeros of `θ_P` in an open annulus are a finite set** — and the\nfactorisation names it: `a ∈ S` and `θ_P a = 0` are the same statement there,\nbecause the multiplicities are positive and the cofactor never vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_factorisation_annulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_factorisation_annulus","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L145-L201","doc":"**`θ_P` factorises over its zeros on any open annulus.**\n\n`exists_finset_factorisation` with `V` the open annulus and `K` the closed one\naround it; `qTheta_pos_of_pos` supplies the non-vanishing point the identity\ntheorem needs, so this carries no hypothesis beyond `0 < c < d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_ne_zero_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_ne_zero_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L139-L143","doc":"`θ_P` is nonzero at every positive real, read in `ℂ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_pos_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_pos_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L124-L137","doc":"**`θ_P` is strictly positive on the positive real axis.**\n\nEvery term of `∑ P^{−n(n+1)} wⁿ` is positive when `w > 0`, so the sum is. This is\nthe cheapest proof that `θ_P` is not identically zero — which is what the\nidentity theorem needs before it will produce a divisor at all, and what the\n`hne` hypotheses below ultimately rest on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_ne_zero_outer","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_ne_zero_outer","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L92-L118","doc":"**Non-vanishing propagates outward across one fundamental annulus.**\n\n`θ_P(P²w) = w·θ_P(w)` with `w ≠ 0`, so a zero on `‖z‖ = P²ρ` would force one on\n`‖z‖ = ρ`. This is why `qTheta_winding_step`'s single hypothesis — no zero on the\n*inner* circle — is enough to make both contours legitimate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.isOpen_openAnnulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.isOpen_openAnnulus","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L81-L86","doc":"An open annulus is open. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.isPreconnected_openAnnulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.isPreconnected_openAnnulus","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean#L44-L79","doc":"**An open annulus in `ℂ` is preconnected.**\n\nIt is the image under `Complex.exp` of the strip `log r < re z < log R`, which is\na preimage of an interval under an `ℝ`-linear map, hence convex.\n\nNeeded because `exists_finset_factorisation` runs the identity theorem, which\nwants a connected domain: an annulus is the smallest such domain containing the\ntwo contour circles of `qTheta_winding_step`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}